#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11647cdd-6315-5718-9425-88f4fb93faa7" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Rational Functions With a Repeated Factor of Degree Higher Than Two"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "043d1ce5-9e3e-5519-9a4c-03569c147664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-\\frac{1}{2x+1}+\\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247455} {:answer/id #uuid "554d505f-19eb-54e9-8916-dac052fc21dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{1}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248193} {:answer/id #uuid "64b65e65-4617-5e48-b1cf-a0372e6395b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{3}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248333} {:answer/id #uuid "7379ce95-99ed-5b20-9302-b338646b620c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{2}{2x+1}+\\frac{1}{2(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248436} {:answer/id #uuid "d091e770-03db-512b-a3b7-40a0f2053ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|-\\frac{1}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "554d505f-19eb-54e9-8916-dac052fc21dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{1}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248193} :answer-field/id #uuid "7aca0b8a-ad9d-5b96-a5a6-ae937ea506b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "513d3507-74c5-5ea9-ac09-4c99e11dedbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-115623" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{A}{2x+1}+\\frac{B}{(2x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{(2x+1)^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo determine the coefficients $A,B,$ and $C,$ we first multiply both sides of the above equation by $(2x+1)^{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}=A(2x+1)^{2}+B(2x+1)+C.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and $B$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =A{(2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+1)}^{2}+B(2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+1)+C \\\\ 1 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, plugging in $x=0$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2} & =A(2(0)+1)^{2}+B(2(0)+1)+C \\\\ 0 & =A+B+1 \\\\ A+B & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, plugging in $x=-1$ and substituting the known value of $C,$ we get an equation relating $A$ and $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-1)^{2} & =A(2(-1)+1)^{2}+B(2(-1)+1)+C \\\\ 4 & =A-B+1 \\\\ A-B & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations $A-B=3$ and $A+B=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2A & =3-1 \\\\ A & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand substituting back into $A+B=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+B & =-1 \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integrand as the partial sum:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{2}{(2x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate this expression term by term to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(2x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{2}{(2x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{(2x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\frac{1}{2x+1}-\\frac{1}{4(2x+1)^{2}}+K,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an integral constant."}]}
